          "The 44.7% renter rate is the critical vulnerability in Mannix's 52% coalition. Renters in this income range are acutely sensitive to housing costs, inflation, and whether their wages are keeping pace with the rising cost of living — and Oregon's housing crisis has created material hardship for exactly this constituency. At $70,427 median income with 44.7% renting, a significant portion of the district's households are spending 30% or more of income on housing, leaving them financially vulnerable to unexpected shocks and dependent on wage growth that has not kept pace with costs. The Hispanic community, which comprises 28% of the district, overlaps significantly with renters and lower-wage earners, and this community's economic concerns — both immigration policy's impact on labor markets and direct household economics — are powerful organizing concerns for Democratic challengers. Mannix's 52% result suggests he is holding enough of the Anglo homeowner majority (62.9% white, presumably with higher ownership rates) to keep the seat, but that coalition is dependent on preventing consolidation of renter and Hispanic voters around a Democratic alternative who can credibly speak to economic pressure. The median age of 36.7 and college attainment of only 23.9% indicate a working-class, not college-educated, constituency — a demographic group that has shown significant volatility in recent electoral cycles and responds to direct messaging about household economics rather than abstract ideology."
