          "The strategic reality for any Democrat holding this seat is that the 21% Republican opposition is not marginal—it represents one-fifth of the district's voters who either prefer Republican governance or are persuadable on specific issues where Democratic messaging has not landed. In a district with high income ($88,342) and high education (43.6% college), that opposition likely centers on tax policy, regulatory burden on small business, public safety in mixed urban-suburban neighborhoods, and the cost of living—not cultural polarization or abstract ideology. Maintaining the 79% requires speaking directly to the material concerns of the 43.6% college-educated and the professional-class renter base: housing affordability for young professionals, education funding that reflects district priorities, and tax policy that does not penalize the higher-income households that anchor the coalition. The Democratic incumbent's task is not to expand further into Republican territory—it is to prevent erosion at the margin by demonstrating that Democratic governance delivers on the economic and quality-of-life concerns that motivated the 79% in the first place.",
