          "The 43.5% renter rate is the most important demographic fact in OR-SD-14 for forward-looking strategy. In a district where median household income is $98,780 — well above state and national medians — a 43.5% renter share indicates a constituency that includes both young professionals early in wealth accumulation and older renters on fixed or moderate incomes, likely concentrated in urban mixed-income neighborhoods. Renters in high-income districts vote differently than renters in low-income districts: they evaluate on housing affordability, transit access, neighborhood safety, and the fiscal health of schools and public services, not on subsistence-level economic anxiety. The incumbent's 86% result suggests effective messaging on these urban-renter priorities; a challenger would need to either outflank on the same issues or risk losing the renter-majority base to low turnout or third-party defection. The college-educated plurality (58.8%) and median age of 39.9 further suggest a constituency that votes on governance competence, environmental policy, and the perceived quality of public institutions — not on cultural symbolism or abstract ideology. Any candidate in this district who frames the race in national partisan terms rather than district-specific governance concerns is leaving persuasion leverage on the table among the college-educated professionals and urban renters who actually decide outcomes here."
